WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.680
The first film showed how a dynein-carried signaling endosome crosses a centimeter

2
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:13.920
in hours. Since a relay could beat diffusion too, how can an experiment tell whether

3
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.480
the receptor actually travels?

4
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.060
Or does it stay at the tip while local signals march home?

5
00:00:21.083 --> 00:00:24.363
Picture two competing models on the same axon.

6
00:00:25.503 --> 00:00:31.143
In the courier model, one active NGF and TrkA endosome rides dynein all the way

7
00:00:31.143 --> 00:00:35.123
home. In the relay model, TrkA stays at the tip.

8
00:00:36.343 --> 00:00:40.283
Like a bucket brigade, each stretch hands a local signal to the next.

9
00:00:41.483 --> 00:00:42.903
Both wake nuclear CREB.

10
00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:48.927
Timing alone cannot choose, because a fast relay can also arrive within hours.

11
00:00:50.287 --> 00:00:53.587
A natural next test is to disable the motor dynein.

12
00:00:54.827 --> 00:01:00.027
Suppose you give NGF only to the far axons, block dynein, and the neurons die.

13
00:01:00.027 --> 00:01:06.087
Predict: does death after blocking dynein prove one vesicle carried the receptor?

14
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.830
No, because both models can need dynein.

15
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:19.150
Blocking it stops a courier endosome from leaving the tip.

16
00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:24.910
But a relay could also use dynein for short local handoffs within each stretch of

17
00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:30.910
axon. If each local step needs dynein, blocking the motor halts the brigade too.

18
00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:33.610
Both models predict death.

19
00:01:34.633 --> 00:01:38.353
To tell two models apart, test an outcome only one predicts.

20
00:01:39.733 --> 00:01:44.513
Instead of stopping dynein everywhere, divide the axon across three sealed chambers:

21
00:01:45.293 --> 00:01:47.293
tip, middle stretch, and cell body.

22
00:01:48.633 --> 00:01:53.473
Now add a drug that blocks TrkA kinase activity only inside the middle chamber.

23
00:01:54.567 --> 00:01:56.307
Watch how the two tracks split.

24
00:01:57.587 --> 00:02:03.047
A relay passing TrkA activity from stretch to stretch hits the inhibitor and dies in

25
00:02:03.047 --> 00:02:06.047
the middle. The courier behaves differently.

26
00:02:07.307 --> 00:02:12.187
Even if the drug quiets its kinase during transit, dynein carries the vesicle

27
00:02:12.187 --> 00:02:14.727
through with NGF still bound inside.

28
00:02:16.027 --> 00:02:19.007
In the drug-free cell body, TrkA can signal again.

29
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:24.610
In 2003 Ye and colleagues ran this test, and survival was spared.

30
00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:29.190
That argues against a propagating wave of TrkA activity.

31
00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:34.950
Still, what if another signal reached the cell body while NGF stayed behind?

32
00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:40.950
Neutralizing NGF once it arrives in the cell body takes survival away.

33
00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:45.390
So the carried ligand matters, though other routes remain open.

34
00:02:46.483 --> 00:02:51.363
Notice why the three-chamber design succeeded where disabling dynein failed.

35
00:02:52.663 --> 00:02:57.463
A perturbation that breaks a shared requirement can never separate rival mechanisms.

36
00:02:58.943 --> 00:03:04.163
When two hypotheses both fit the timing, aim your test at the exact step where their

37
00:03:04.163 --> 00:03:05.323
predictions diverge.
